    "body": "Problem:\r\n`vim.on_key()` callbacks are invoked for keys that `getchar()` consumes,\r\nwhich never reach the main loop, and there is no way to tell such a key\r\napart.  With ui2 the `on_key` handler activated after interactive\r\ncmdline, handles it as `pager_char` (thus enters the ui2 pager).\r\n\r\nSolution:\r\nSkip `vim.on_key()` callbacks during `getchar()`/`getcharstr()`.\r\n\r\nfix #40010\r\n",
    "closed_at": "2026-08-24T14:18:48Z",
    "comment_data": [],
    "comment_regular_data": [
        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "After this PR, this is still another edge case where `on_key` \"misses\": a `:s///c` prompt still lets the key (y/n/...) passthrough:\r\n\r\n    :s///c reply -> y\r\n\r\n\r\nKey callouts:\r\n\r\n1. the three exceptions (recursive reads, `<Cmd>` payloads, getchar) to the `on_key` \"contract\" are all instances of one unstated rule: keys entering the main command loop.\r\n2. ui2 is asking the wrong primitive.\r\n\r\nTODO:\r\n- ~~ui2 can leverage `CmdAtom` to answer the question inherently.~~\r\n    - Needs `CmdAtomPre` to detect, not `CmdAtom`. *Or* `v:cmdatom_id` ...\r\n- But this PR is still worthwhile. Even if ui2 migrates to `CmdAtom[Pre]`, `pager_char` still needs to consume the key, which a deferred event (`CmdAtom`) can't do. So a narrow `on_key` handler is still needed (with `getchar`-suppression, i.e. this PR).\r\n- Followup PR which fixes the `:s///c` case.\r\n",
            "created_at": "2026-08-24T13:36:14Z",
